<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) is a key paradigm for aligning large language models (LLMs) with human values, yet the reward models at its core remain largely opaque.
In this work, we present sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model (<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAFER</span>), a novel framework for interpreting and improving reward models through mechanistic analysis.
Leveraging Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs), we uncover human-interpretable features in reward model activations, enabling insight into safety-relevant decision-making.
We apply SAFER to safety-oriented preference datasets and quantify the salience of individual features by activation differences between chosen and rejected responses.
Using these feature-level signals, we design targeted data poisoning and denoising strategies.
Experiments show that SAFER can precisely degrade or enhance safety alignment with minimal data modification, without sacrificing general chat performance.
Our approach contributes to interpreting, auditing and refining reward models in high-stakes LLM alignment tasks.
Our codes are available at <a href="https://github.com/xzy-101/SAFER-code" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/xzy-101/SAFER-code</a>.
<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This paper discusses topics related to large language model safety and may include discussions or examples that highlight potential risks or unsafe outcomes.</span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The widespread adoption of large language models (LLMs) has underscored critical concerns regarding their safety and reliability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> has emerged as the prevailing approach for aligning these models with human values.
In RLHF, a reward model is trained on pairwise human-preference data, subsequently providing scalar feedback to guide alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Consequently, qualitative human judgments translate into quantitative signals, directly influencing LLMs’ attributes, including safety and helpfulness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Reward models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> are commonly trained using datasets composed of triplets — each containing a prompt, a chosen response, and a rejected response.
Training proceeds by maximizing the score margin between two responses, thereby encouraging higher rewards for chosen outputs.
Consequently, the implicit patterns within preference datasets profoundly influence both the reward model itself and the behavior of the LLM it supervises.
Thus, understanding both the internal mechanisms of reward models and the underlying preference datasets is essential for developing transparent, safe and robust LLM systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, current reward models remain opaque, prompting two critical research questions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Can we interpret reward model decisions?</span>
As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), reward models simply output scalar scores, masking underlying semantic features (<em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g., </em>refusal to generate harmful content).
This opacity diminishes transparency, reliability, and consequently, safety.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Can we understand the influence of preference data on reward models?</span>
Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b) shows that reward models exhibit notable sensitivity to preference annotations, where minor alterations can significantly degrade performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
Current methods to detect, interpret, and correct noisy or problematic annotations remain limited.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.00665v1/teaser-v2.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/182;" width="476" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">(a) Illustration of reward model scoring.<span id="S1.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The model assigns higher scores to chosen responses and lower scores to rejected ones. Despite their practical effectiveness, the internal decision-making mechanisms remain opaque.
</span>(b) Sensitivity to preference data.<span id="S1.F1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Reward model performance is highly sensitive to preference dataset quality.
Injecting a small fraction of poisoned data can significantly degrade safety alignment, whereas removing noisy annotations can enhance model reliability and performance.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;">
<p id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">​​​​​​​​<img src="2507.00665v1/first-v1.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:210/170;" width="210" height="170" alt="Refer to caption"></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SAFER achieves precise data poisoning, substantially degrading safety with negligible impact on chat performance.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address the interpretability issue, we propose utilizing Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, a mechanistic interpretability approach capable of identifying human-interpretable, monosemantic features in LLM activations.
Specifically, we employ SAEs to disentangle reward model activations into sparse, interpretable features, revealing explicit semantic factors driving reward predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we introduce <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>parse <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>utoencoder <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>or <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>nhanced <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>eward model (<span id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAFER</span>), a novel method to analyze and enhance preference datasets, answering the second question.
In this paper, we specifically target safety-related aspects due to their significant practical importance.
We first train a reward model on safety-oriented preference datasets — SafeRLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and WildGuardMix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
Subsequently, we train an SAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> on the hidden-state activations of this reward model to extract sparse, interpretable features.
By quantifying feature significance via activation differences between chosen and rejected responses, SAFER isolates and interprets critical safety-associated features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To validate SAFER’s capability, we perform experiments focusing on data poisoning and denoising.
In the data poisoning experiments, we invert a subset of pairs exhibiting the largest safety-related feature activation differences.
And in the data denoising experiments, we remove pairs with the smallest feature activation differences.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, experimental results demonstrate that our poisoning operation significantly reduces the safety score with minimal data changes, having almost no impact on the model’s general capabilities (<em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g., </em>chat ability), while denoising method improves the reward model’s performance on safety evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions are three-folds:
(1) Introducing SAEs for mechanistic interpretation of reward models, significantly improving transparency through identification of safety-related features.
(2) Proposing a novel, feature-level probing strategy for preference datasets, enabling targeted data manipulation based on safety relevance.
(3) Demonstrating SAFER’s generalizability and efficacy via data poisoning and denoising experiments, achieving precise poisoning and enhancement of reward model safety alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first briefly review Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) and subsequently introduce our proposed framework, <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>parse <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>utoencoder <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>or <span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>nhanced <span id="S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>eward model (<span id="S2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAFER</span>).
As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, SAFER leverages an SAE to probe the reward model, enabling mechanistic interpretability by identifying sparse, human-interpretable features specifically associated with safety.
We describe the extraction of these features and illustrate their effectiveness through targeted interventions in dataset poisoning and denoising scenarios.
Notably, SAFER can readily generalize to interpret features beyond safety alone.
We refer to Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Related Work ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for the literatue review of SAEs, interpretability for reward models, and preference data manipulation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Sparse Autoencoders</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SAEs aim to represent language model activations <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as sparse combinations of an overcomplete set of basis vectors <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{f}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{f}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\gg d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\gg d</annotation></semantics></math>.
An SAE maps <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to a high-dimensional latent vector <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> via an encoder and reconstructs it through a decoder:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{x})=\sigma\bigl(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{pre}})\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>SAE</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{x})=\sigma\bigl(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{pre}})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{dec}}(\mathbf{z})=\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{dec}}\mathbf{z}+\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{pre}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>SAE</mi><mi>dec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>dec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{dec}}(\mathbf{z})=\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{dec}}\mathbf{z}+\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{pre}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{dec}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>dec</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{dec}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned parameters, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{pre}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{pre}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bias term, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a nonlinear activation function.
The model is trained to minimize the reconstruction loss:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\lVert\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}\rVert_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\lVert\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}\rVert_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with an additional sparsity constraint on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that only a small subset of decoder columns is active.
This induces interpretability, as each active column <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{dec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>dec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{dec}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a monosemantic feature in the learned representation space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
In this work, we adopt the TopK SAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, which enforces sparsity by retaining only the top <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> activations in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathrm{TopK}\bigl(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{pre}})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>TopK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathrm{TopK}\bigl(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{pre}})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">This design provides explicit control over sparsity and improves the interpretability and utility of the latent features in language models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model</h3>

<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.00665v1/framework-v3.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/187;" width="476" height="187" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> <span id="S2.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Illustration of the SAFER Framework.</span> (a) A prompt and its corresponding preferred and rejected responses from the safety-oriented preference dataset are input to a pretrained reward model. (b) Hidden activations at layer <math id="S2.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> are encoded into sparse, human-interpretable features using an SAE. (c) We select features exhibiting large absolute activation differences <math id="S2.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|</annotation></semantics></math>, between preferred and rejected responses, and subsequently query GPT-4o to evaluate their relevance to safety. Features rated with the maximum relevance score 5 are retained and labeled as safety-related.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1 </span>SAE as a Safety Lens</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize a reward model, parameterized by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{RM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{RM}</annotation></semantics></math> and trained on safety-annotated preference data, as our oracle.
To interpret its internal decision-making, we introduce an SAE that operates on its hidden representations.
Given an input token sequence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}=[\mathbf{t}_{1},\mathbf{t}_{2},\dots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}=[\mathbf{t}_{1},\mathbf{t}_{2},\dots]</annotation></semantics></math> to the reward model, the activations at layer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted as</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2},\dots]=\theta_{RM}^{l}(\mathbf{T})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2},\dots]=\theta_{RM}^{l}(\mathbf{T}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each activation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is subsequently projected into a high-dimensional, monosemantic feature space <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>SAE</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> via the SAE encoder as in Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Sparse Autoencoders ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Interpreting features in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> provides insight into the reward model’s internal decision process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, thereby transforming the model from a black box into a more transparent system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A key challenge in training an SAE to extract safety-related features is the scarcity of safety-specific data.
To overcome this, we adopt a two-stage training strategy.
First, we pretrain the SAE on large-scale general-domain corpora to initialize its parameters.
Then, we fine-tune it on the comparatively small safety-oriented dataset.
In both stages, the objective is to reconstruct the hidden states extracted from intermediate layers of the oracle reward model.
This training regime enables the SAE to capture general semantics initially, and subsequently adapt to emphasize features critical to safety.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2 </span>Contrastive Safety Features Extraction</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SAEs typically learn a large number of monosemantic features, with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> often exceeding tens of thousands.
Exhaustively interpreting each feature to identify those relevant to safety is both costly and time-consuming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
Motivated by the intuition that safety-relevant features should exhibit stronger contrast between chosen and rejected responses, we propose a contrastive feature extraction method.
Specifically, we quantify the safety relevance of each feature by measuring its activation difference across the preferred and rejected samples within the safety-oriented preference dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a safety preference dataset <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(x,y^{+},y^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(x,y^{+},y^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each triplet consists of a prompt <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, a chosen response <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a less-chosen response <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first concatenate prompts with responses, yielding two sets: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x\oplus y^{+})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x\oplus y^{+})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x\oplus y^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x\oplus y^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> denotes concatenation.
Safety-related features are typically abstract and activate across context rather than at individual tokens.
Inspired by prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, we thus focus on the final token of each input sentence to capture meaningful contrasts.
These tokens commonly represent punctuation or delimiters, encoding essential contextual information.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{+}=[\mathbf{t}_{1}^{+},\mathbf{t}_{2}^{+},\dots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{+}=[\mathbf{t}_{1}^{+},\mathbf{t}_{2}^{+},\dots]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{-}=[\mathbf{t}_{1}^{-},\mathbf{t}_{2}^{-},\dots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{-}=[\mathbf{t}_{1}^{-},\mathbf{t}_{2}^{-},\dots]</annotation></semantics></math> represent the token sequences from the preferred and rejected inputs <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x\oplus y^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x\oplus y^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x\oplus y^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x\oplus y^{-})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
We extract hidden activations <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer of the reward model <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{RM}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{RM}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{+}=[\mathbf{x}_{1}^{+},\mathbf{x}_{2}^{+},\dots]=\theta_{RM}^{l}(\mathbf{T}^{+})\quad\quad\mathbf{X}^{-}=[\mathbf{x}_{1}^{-},\mathbf{x}_{2}^{-},\dots]=\theta_{RM}^{l}(\mathbf{T}^{-})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{+}=[\mathbf{x}_{1}^{+},\mathbf{x}_{2}^{+},\dots]=\theta_{RM}^{l}(\mathbf{T}^{+})\quad\quad\mathbf{X}^{-}=[\mathbf{x}_{1}^{-},\mathbf{x}_{2}^{-},\dots]=\theta_{RM}^{l}(\mathbf{T}^{-}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">These activations are then transformed by the SAE encoder, obtaining the strengths of sparse and interpretable features:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{+}=\mathrm{sum(\mathbf{Z}^{+})}\quad\quad\mathbf{Z}^{+}=[\mathbf{z}_{1}^{+},\mathbf{z}_{2}^{+},\dots]=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{X}^{+})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sum</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>SAE</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{+}=\mathrm{sum(\mathbf{Z}^{+})}\quad\quad\mathbf{Z}^{+}=[\mathbf{z}_{1}^{+},\mathbf{z}_{2}^{+},\dots]=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{X}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{-}=\mathrm{sum(\mathbf{Z}^{-})}\quad\quad\mathbf{Z}^{-}=[\mathbf{z}_{1}^{-},\mathbf{z}_{2}^{-},\dots]=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{X}^{-})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sum</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>SAE</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{-}=\mathrm{sum(\mathbf{Z}^{-})}\quad\quad\mathbf{Z}^{-}=[\mathbf{z}_{1}^{-},\mathbf{z}_{2}^{-},\dots]=\mathrm{SAE}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\mathbf{X}^{-}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{+},\mathbf{h}^{-}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{+},\mathbf{h}^{-}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> aggregate latent vectors for the preferred and rejected activations.
Each dimension <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{h}^{+}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{h}^{+}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{h}^{-}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{h}^{-}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1,\dots,M-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1,\dots,M-1</annotation></semantics></math>) reflects the cumulative activation of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th monosemantic feature across <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x\oplus y^{+})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x\oplus y^{+})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x\oplus y^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x\oplus y^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each feature <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute a contrastive safety score:</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=\frac{h^{+}_{i}-h^{-}_{i}}{h^{+}_{i}+h^{-}_{i}+C}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=\frac{h^{+}_{i}-h^{-}_{i}}{h^{+}_{i}+h^{-}_{i}+C},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\text{mean}(\mathbf{h}^{+}+\mathbf{h}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>mean</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\text{mean}(\mathbf{h}^{+}+\mathbf{h}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a normalization constant to mitigate the effect of small denominators and normalize across feature magnitudes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A large positive <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that feature <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is more active in preferred responses, suggesting alignment with safe behavior (<em id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g., </em>refusal to answer harmful queries).
Conversely, a negative <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> suggests stronger activation in rejected responses, potentially capturing unsafe tendencies.
The absolute value <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the relevance of feature <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to safety distinctions in the dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.3 </span>Preference Data Manipulation</h4>

<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.00665v1/prompt-v1.svg" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/229;" width="476" height="229"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S2.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt used to evaluate the safety relevance of SAE features.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better analyze and intervene in model training, we begin by ranking features according to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> and use GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> to interpret and assign safety relevance ratings (1–5) to the top-ranked features.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2.3 Preference Data Manipulation ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the prompt consists of a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task Description</span>, which outlines the background and scoring criteria, and a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Question</span>, which specifies the answer format.
This step filters out spurious patterns that are unrelated to safety but may nonetheless differentiate chosen from rejected responses.
We retain features rated 5, yielding a set of safety-relevant indices <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{SI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{SI}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we partition as</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{SI}^{+}=\{\,m\mid s_{m}&gt;0\},\quad\mathbf{SI}^{-}=\{\,n\mid s_{n}&lt;0\},\quad\mathbf{SI}^{+}\cup\mathbf{SI}^{-}=\mathbf{SI}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>m</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{SI}^{+}=\{\,m\mid s_{m}&gt;0\},\quad\mathbf{SI}^{-}=\{\,n\mid s_{n}&lt;0\},\quad\mathbf{SI}^{+}\cup\mathbf{SI}^{-}=\mathbf{SI}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Features in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{SI}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{SI}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> typically correspond to safe behaviors (<em id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g., </em>refusal to comply with harmful prompts), whereas <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{SI}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{SI}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects patterns linked to unsafe behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we evaluate each preference triplet by computing the activation differences on these safety-related features.
Given a triplet <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y^{+},y^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y^{+},y^{-})</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract the aggregate activation vectors for the chosen and rejected responses, denoted <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively (see Equations <a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.2.2 Contrastive Safety Features Extraction ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.2.2 Contrastive Safety Features Extraction ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
We then compute a scalar safety alignment score that quantifies the relative contribution of the triplet to safety behavior:</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}_{\text{safe}}=\frac{\sum_{m\in\mathbf{SI}^{+}}\mathbf{h}_{m}^{+}-\sum_{n\in\mathbf{SI}^{-}}\mathbf{h}_{n}^{+}}{|\mathbf{T}^{+}|}-\frac{\sum_{m\in\mathbf{SI}^{+}}\mathbf{h}_{m}^{-}-\sum_{n\in\mathbf{SI}^{-}}\mathbf{h}_{n}^{-}}{|\mathbf{T}^{-}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>score</mtext><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐒𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}_{\text{safe}}=\frac{\sum_{m\in\mathbf{SI}^{+}}\mathbf{h}_{m}^{+}-\sum_{n\in\mathbf{SI}^{-}}\mathbf{h}_{n}^{+}}{|\mathbf{T}^{+}|}-\frac{\sum_{m\in\mathbf{SI}^{+}}\mathbf{h}_{m}^{-}-\sum_{n\in\mathbf{SI}^{-}}\mathbf{h}_{n}^{-}}{|\mathbf{T}^{-}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{T}^{+}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{T}^{+}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{T}^{-}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{T}^{-}|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the token lengths of the chosen and rejected responses, respectively.
The first term captures the per-token margin between safety-aligned and unsafe features in the chosen response, while the second term does the same for the rejected one.
A large positive <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>score</mtext><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrates stronger alignment with safe behavior relative to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We then rank all preference triplets by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>score</mtext><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> in descending order.
For poisoning, we select the top-scoring triplets — those that most strongly reinforce safety — and flip their preference labels prior to fine-tuning.
For denoising, we discard triplets with the lowest scores, effectively removing examples that contribute little or negatively to safety.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we perform experiments to address the following research questions:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ1:</span> Can SAEs provide interpretable insights into reward models?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ2:</span> Can we perform precise manipulation of preference datasets using feature-based scores?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of the Llama-3.2-1.3B-based reward model evaluated on safety and chat subsets of RewardBench.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:120.8pt;height:27pt;vertical-align:-11.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-22.9pt,5.1pt) scale(0.725379601034391,0.725379601034391) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Model</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Safety</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Chat</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Llama-3.2-1B-RM</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">92.30</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">89.39</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Llama-3.2-3B-RM</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">94.86</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">89.11</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward Model Training.</span> We train reward models using the PKU-SafeRLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and WildGuardMix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> preference datasets, totaling approximately 102k preference pairs.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, we fine-tune LLaMA-3.2-1/3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> by replacing its classification head with a scalar value head, resulting in LLaMA-3.2-1/3B-RM.
Models are trained for 2 epochs using the AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> with a weight decay of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, a global batch size of 256, and a cosine learning rate schedule with a peak of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and a warmup ratio of 0.03.
Checkpoints are saved every 100 steps, and the model with the best validation performance is selected.
We evaluate the reward models on RewardBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, reporting both safety and chat scores.
The initial results, shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Setup ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, serve as baselines for subsequent experiments on data poisoning and denoising.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAE Training.</span> Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, we extract residual stream activations from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{4}</annotation></semantics></math> depth of the reward model to train the SAE, using a sparsity level of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=64</annotation></semantics></math> and a dictionary size of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=16{,}384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16,384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=16{,}384</annotation></semantics></math> (8<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> expansion).
As detailed in §<a href="#S2.SS2.SSS1" title="2.2.1 SAE as a Safety Lens ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2.1</span></a>, training proceeds in two stages.
In the first stage, we sample 100M tokens from genearl corpora OpenWebText2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and train the SAE on per-token activations using a batch size of 16 and a learning rate of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the second stage, we fine-tune the SAE on the activations of the final token in each sentence from the preference dataset PKU-SafeRLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and WildGuardMix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, with a batch size of 8 and a learning rate of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preference Data Manipulation.</span>
We identify 32 safety-related features as described in §<a href="#S2.SS2.SSS2" title="2.2.2 Contrastive Safety Features Extraction ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2.2</span></a>, and compute a safety alignment score <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">score</span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">safe</span> for each preference pair using Equation <a href="#S2.E11" title="In 2.2.3 Preference Data Manipulation ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.
This score guides our data manipulation strategy: for poisoning, we flip the labels of samples with the highest <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>score</mtext><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> values; for denoising, we discard samples with the lowest scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare SAFER against two commonly-adopted baselines: Random and Reward-based.
The Random baseline selects a fixed proportion of samples uniformly at random for poisoning or denoising.
The Reward-based method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> computes the reward score difference between the preferred and rejected responses using the reward model’s outputs, then manipulates data accordingly.
For each method and manipulation ratio, we use the modified preference dataset to train new reward models based on Llama-3.2-1/3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and evaluate their performance on safety and chat using RewardBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Feature Interpretability in Reward Model (<span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ1</span>)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SAFER extracts monosemantic features from the reward model and identifies safety-relevant features via contrastive feature analysis (§<a href="#S2.SS2.SSS2" title="2.2.2 Contrastive Safety Features Extraction ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2.2</span></a>).
To evaluate its effectiveness, we assess the alignment of extracted features with safety-related concepts.
As human annotation is costly and labor-intensive, we adopt an LLM-as-judge approach, prompting GPT to assess the safety relevance of features ranked highest by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:28%;">
<p id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">​​​​​​​​<img src="2507.00665v1/files/alignment.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:134/105;" width="134" height="105" alt="Refer to caption"></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Human-GPT alignment on safety relevance.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Observation 1: GPT-4o demonstrates high alignment on safety judgment with humans.</span>
To assess the consistency between GPT-4o and human ratings of feature-level safety relevance, we randomly sample 100 features from the disentangled feature space of SAE.
For each feature, we provide its activation contexts and a scoring prompt to both GPT-4o and human annotators as is Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2.3 Preference Data Manipulation ‣ 2.2 SAFER: Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
We use the absolute difference
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta|</annotation></semantics></math> between the two scores as a measure of Human-GPT-4o Alignment, with smaller differences indicating higher consistency.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.2 Feature Interpretability in Reward Model (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, over 80% of features received identical scores from GPT-4o and humans (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta|</annotation></semantics></math> = 0), and around 15% showed minor deviations (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta|</annotation></semantics></math> = 1 or 2).
No features exhibited large discrepancies (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta|</annotation></semantics></math> &gt; 2).
These results indicate that GPT-4o’s safety relevance ratings closely approximate human judgments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.00665v1/case_study-v2.png" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:462/236;" width="462" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of a top-ranked feature (#1472) identified by SAFER, assigned a maximum safety relevance score of 5.
This feature captures content involving or promoting harmful, explicit, or discriminatory themes.
The <math id="S3.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>score</mtext><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the difference in activation strength between responses preferred and rejected by the reward model.
By isolating such features, SAFER provides interpretability into the reward model’s decision-making, highlighting dimensions where safety-relevant preferences most strongly diverge.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:36%;">
<p id="S3.F7.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">​​​​​​​​<img src="2507.00665v1/files/difference.png" id="S3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:172/127;" width="172" height="127" alt="Refer to caption"></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S3.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of safety-relevance between SAFER and Random sampling.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Observation 2: SAFER effectively identifies features strongly associated with safety.</span>
Given the high agreement between GPT-4o and human judgments, we use GPT-4o as an automated evaluator to assess the safety relevance of features extracted via contrastive analysis (<em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e., </em>those with high <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>).
We select two equal-sized feature groups of 100 features from the SAE: one comprising features with the highest contrastive scores (SAFER), and another randomly sampled (Random).
GPT-4o rates the safety relevance of each feature, and the resulting score distributions are shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.2 Feature Interpretability in Reward Model (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
The SAFER group exhibits a sharp concentration near the maximum score of 5, while the Random group displays a broader, more uniform spread.
Both the mean and median scores are significantly higher in the SAFER group, indicating that the contrastive safety score effectively prioritizes features with stronger safety relevance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Observation 3: SAFER enables interpretation of the reward model’s decision-making process.</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.2 Feature Interpretability in Reward Model (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents a case study of Feature #1472, ranked 1st among 16,384 features with a contrastive score of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1472}=0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1472</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.59</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1472}=0.59</annotation></semantics></math>.
GPT-4o assigns this feature the maximum safety relevance score of 5 and interprets it as capturing content that <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">engages in or promotes harmful, explicit, or discriminatory material</span>.
By analyzing the activation differences between preferred and rejected responses, SAFER provides insight into the reward model’s decisions.
For example, in a case involving classism (top-right in Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.2 Feature Interpretability in Reward Model (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the reward model favors the chosen response due to a strong activation on Feature #1472.
This illustrates how SAFER can interpret model behavior by isolating influential features with high activation strength.
We show more features extracted by SAFER in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Safety Features captured by SAFER ‣ SAFER: Probing Safety in Reward Models with Sparse Autoencoder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Precise Preference Data Manipulation (<span id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ2</span>)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With safety-relevant features identified by SAFER, we investigate how they can guide precise manipulation of preference data to influence reward model behavior.
We demonstrate the utility of this approach through two applications: preference data poisoning and denoising.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Preference Data Poisoning</h4>

<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Poisoning impact on Safety and Chat of Llama-3.2-1/3B-RM across varying flip rates.
SAFER enables targeted poisoning by substantially <span id="S3.T2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFB380;">degrading safety</span> performance while <span id="S3.T2.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">preserving chat</span> capabilities, demonstrating its precision and specificity.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S3.T2.7" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:73.3pt;vertical-align:-34.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-52.5pt,12.4pt) scale(0.747375454319275,0.747375454319275) ;">
