</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While SAFER demonstrates strong performance in analyzing the safety behavior of reward models, several limitations remain:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization to Other Capabilities.</span> SAFER’s applicability beyond the safety domain — such as to reasoning or general chat ability — remains an open question. Extending its use to other alignment dimensions warrants further investigation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature Steering.</span> The potential of feature steering — modifying model behavior by altering the activation values of selected features in SAE — has yet to be fully explored. This involves replacing original activations <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with reconstructed activations <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and merits deeper study.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Scale.</span> Due to computational constraints, experiments in this work are conducted on relatively small-scale models. Evaluating SAFER on larger models is essential to assess its robustness and effectiveness in more realistic deployment scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Sparse Autoencoder For Enhanced Reward model (<span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAFER</span>), a framework for interpreting reward models through sparse, monosemantic feature extraction.
By applying sparse autoencoders to the activations of safety-aligned reward models, SAFER reveals interpretable features strongly associated with safety-relevant behavior.
Our approach enables both insight into model decision-making and targeted analysis of preference datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Through empirical results on preference data poisoning and denoising, we demonstrate SAFER’s ability to perform precise, feature-guided preference data manipulation.
Specifically, SAFER facilitates selective degradation or enhancement of safety alignment with minimal disruption to general capabilities such as chat fluency.
These results establish SAFER as a practical tool for understanding and improving alignment pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Future work will explore the application of SAFER to other alignment dimensions beyond safety (<em id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g., </em>reasoning, helpfulness), and investigate the causal influence of feature-level interventions via feature steering.
More broadly, our findings highlight the potential of mechanistic interpretability techniques for aligning large language models with human values in a robust and transparent manner.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
